---
import SaidLayout from "../../../layouts/SaidLayout.astro";
import SaidPlaceholder from "../../../components/SaidPlaceholder.astro";
---

<SaidLayout
  title="The keyboard is the desk — Said guides"
  description="Every Said flow without the mouse: moving between cards, opening them, filing them, finding them."
  active="guides"
>
  <article class="said-article">
    <h1>The keyboard is the desk</h1>
    <p class="said-meta">One rule, a handful of keys, no modes.</p>

    <p>
      Said has one keyboard rule and it never bends: a key goes to the topmost
      screen and nowhere else. No global shortcuts, no hidden modes, no key that
      reaches past what you're looking at. When a text field has focus, it eats
      the keys — that's the whole system.
    </p>

    <h2>Moving around</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>arrows</code> or <code>h j k l</code> — walk the cards and lanes.
      </li>
      <li><code>enter</code> — open the card under the cursor.</li>
      <li><code>esc</code> — close the topmost screen, always.</li>
    </ul>
    <SaidPlaceholder label="Capture — cursor walking the board" />

    <h2>Making and moving</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><code>n</code> — a new card.</li>
      <li>
        Move a card between lanes from its own screen — or drag it; the mouse
        can do everything the keys can.
      </li>
      <li>Resolving asks how it ended: done, abandoned, or not relevant.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Finding</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>/</code> — the finder: exact words and meaning, side by side.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>?</code> — the key help, which only ever shows keys that work right
        now.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <SaidPlaceholder label="Capture — the finder" />

    <p>
      If a key is shown on screen, it works. If it isn't, it doesn't. You can
      trust your hands to learn the desk in a day.
    </p>
  </article>
</SaidLayout>
